				Id:"Mt-Fuji",
				Description: "<p>Fujiyama as it is known in the local language, translating to 'Wisteria Mountain', is a volcano and seemingly spring island in the New World.</p><p>It is located quite close to the Northern Calm Belt of the New World, somewhere around the middle/last stretch of the sea. As a colossal volcanic mountain that sprouts out of the sea, Mt. Fuji has a large foothill that narrows down into two peaks that stretch high into the skies. Below its sea level is a large reservoir of lava pertaining to the still active volcano.</p><p>Originally, the Wano Country was built at the foothills of the volcano at sea level. However, at the end of the Void Century, massive stone walls were erected to surround the entire country in order to protect it from the World Government. This however caused rainwater to accumulate within its walls, and thus started drowning the island like a giant cauldron. The people of the country were forced to relocate up close to the peak of Mount Fuji that peeked over the walls; several entire islands were taken and placed around the peak of the volcano almost like a jigsaw puzzle, thus allowing for new land at the top that wouldn't be drowned, where the new Wano Country was built.</p><p>Each island has its own unique climate due to being of seemingly different origin. While Mt. Fuji is seemingly a spring island, the other islands stretch across all seasons, resulting in wildly different weather depending on where in the country you are. The description of this country built of islands stolen from other places matches perfectly with the legend of Oars, the Country Puller, said to have gathered together islands from other places of the world and assembled them all together to create a single country. The fact that a large skull in his image was erected on what is now called Onigashima, the smaller of the volcano peaks, could further suggest a connection, however at the moment this has not been confirmed officially in any form.</p><p>The remaining water flushes out from some of the edges of the wall, in the form of large waterfalls. The country can be entered through these waterfalls by riding the large koi fish that swim upcurrent, though this method is incredibly dangerous. An easier entrance is via the Mogura port, hidden behind one of the waterfalls at sea level and leading up to the country via an artificially built elevator, though it is heavily regulated, making the Wano Country a naturally impregnable fortress.</p><p>With that said, these measures were only enacted to protect from the World Government, and the ultimate desire of the people of Wano is to dissolve these borders and destroy the walls when the time is right. This can seemingly only be achieved by the giant elephant Zunesha, who can only be commanded by Momonosuke, current shogun of Wano. By doing this, the ancient weapon Pluton, an ancient battleship capable of wiping an entire island in one blast, can be released upon the world, as the weapon is said to be hiding beneath sea level under the mountain, and it is believed releasing it by destroying the borders will help pave the path towards the Dawn of the World.</p><p>Like most of the Wano Country, Mt. Fuji is inspired by the real life Mt. Fuji in Japan, though it is written with a different kanji; while the original Mt. Fuji stands for 'wealth', this Mt. Fuji refers to the wisteria tree.</p>",
