				Class: "tiny",
				Id:"Wano-Country",
				Description: "<p>The Wano Country is a non-Government independent shogunate located atop the volcano island of Mt. Fuji.</p><p>Originally built on the foothills of the mountain at sea level, it eventually relocated close to the volcano peak when massive walls erected around the island caused it to become a basin of water. The Country is divided into five islands artificially placed around the summit of Mt. Fuji, upon which rests its capital acting as a central sixth island as part of Mt. Fuji.</p><p>Wano has a very unique and peculiar culture, one that has remained fairly unchanged and unaffected by the outside world for centuries due to its isolationist policy. Honor is valued quite highly in the country, and its knights, known by the name of 'samurai', follow a strict code of chivalry. Similarly, its 'ninja' are known for being stealthy warriors operating like a form of secret services. Wano uses its own currency, silver, gold, and platinum, and has a particularly unique cuisine built on seafood and local ingredients, including oden, soba, sushi, donburi, and so much more. The country is also rich in minerals, having developed a strong stonemasonry and swordsmithing culture (later exploited by Kaidou to build weapon factories on the island). As such Wano is also the country of origin of kairoseki, the condensated seastone made of the magmatic pyrobloin particles secreted by the Fuji volcano.</p><p>During the Void Century 800 years ago, the Wano Country was ruled by the Kouzuki Shogunate, who were allied with the ancient kingdom of old, and used their expertise in stonemasonry to craft the poneglyphs to help them retain their ideals. After erecting massive walls around the country, they would then adopt an isolationist policy by closing their borders from the world, making entering and leaving the country illegal for their protection. Soon after or sometime after this, the World Government attempted to seize the island, which around this time was known as a 'Gold Country', but were stopped by Shimotsuki Ryuma, a legendary swordsman who was said to have killed a dragon over the skies of the Wano Capital. For this he was venerated as a 'Sword God' and made a national treasure.</p><p>The Kouzuki Family (translating to 'moonlight') would rule the country for eight centuries as shogun, with each island being ruled by one of five Daimyo, vassal lords of the shogun, composed by five families: the Shimotsuki Clan ('Ice Moon'), the Fuugetsu Clan ('Wind Moon'), the Uzuki Clan ('Rain Moon'), the Amatsuki Clan ('Sky Moon'), and the Kurozumi Clan ('Black Charcoal'). 85 years ago, Daimyo Kurozumi attempted a coup when there was no Kouzuki successor, but his plot was foiled and his clan persecuted as a result. The Amatsuki Clan also seemingly vanished during this coup, resulting in the Shimotsuki clan governing over both the regions of Ringo and Hakumai, and Kuri becoming a lawless region, leaving only three families ruling the islands. The persecution of the Kurozumi would in turn then lead to Daimyo Kurozumi's grandson Orochi developing a great grudge and ultimately stealing the throne of the country in an elaborate coup by teaming up with the pirate Kaidou. The Wano Country thus went from being a secluded paradise into a hellish dictatorship, overrun with slavery and poverty. However, despite killing the heir Kouzuki Oden, his vassals, the 'Akazaya' Nine Red Scabbards, and his children survived, and after 20 long years set an assault with foreign pirates and the new Joyboy to liberate the country in the Raid of Onigashima. Thus the country was free once more under the rule of the current shogun Kouzuki Momonosuke.</p><p>The entire culture of the country, down to its isolationism, is directly lifted from that of Japan, though some may not be aware of the fact that even geographically the country mimics Japan. When seen on a map from the proper cardinal direction, each of the islands matches one of the different islands or regions of Japan. Onigashima is meant to represent Hokkaido, despite being located on the side. Even the name of 'Wa no Country' was one of the original names of Japan, meaning the 'Country of Harmony'.</p>",
