				Description: "<p>Impel Down is a colossal building located in the middle of the sea of the lower Calm Belt, connected through the Tarai Current to Marine Headquarters and Enies Lobby</p><p>Serving as the World's Government greatest prison for keeping the world's most dangerous criminals, referred by some simply as the 'Great Prison' or the 'Underwater Prison', Impel Down is a cylindrical towering fortress that is built on the sea floor, stretching up so high that it barely reaches the sea level at the top. Divided into six total levels under the water (and a ground floor above it), each descending circle offers tortures more vicious than the last, with inhumane conditions that has led many to call it 'Hell' itself.</p><p>The prison is considered incredibly difficult to escape from, as being located in the middle of the Calm Belt, even those capable enough to escape from its many layers of security would find themselves stranded in a windless sea surrounded by Sea Kings. In its history, there have only been three breakouts: one over 100 years ago by Morley, who managed to dig out through the powers of her push push fruit, digging a tunnel out of the prison, though none but Morley are aware of this fact; second, Shiki the Golden Lion who escaped with the powers of his Float Float fruit after cutting off his legs; and third, the breakout led by Luffy after breaking into the prison to save Ace, during which Blackbeard also broke out several dangerous criminals.</p><p>This old fortress can be descended through either a heavily guarded lift which goes all the way from the top to Level 6, or through a set of stairs, though these alternate to hinder prisoners on the lower levels. Particularly, a set of stairs go from Level 1 to Level 4, and then different sets of stairs connect to Level 5, with one of them going all the way to Level 6. The prison's symbol is a super imposed I and D, standing for 'Impel Down'.</p><p>Impel Down's design is derived from Dante Alighieri's depiction of Inferno, Christian Hell, in the famous Divine Comedy, depicting 'Hell' as a set of circles or levels that descend and grow progressively more painful, matching similar motifs such as the bottom of Hell being frozen over. The idea of an isolated prison at sea could also have been inspired by the famous Alcatraz.</p>",
